=============================================================================
WHAT THIS CARD ACTUALLY HAD LEFT TO DO
=============================================================================

Four of the six boxes were already true when the card was opened, from work
done under earlier cards. Each was verified rather than assumed:

  run-tests.ps1 green, both suites   already true
  P1-12/13/14 as automated tests     already true - StockDecrementTests
                                     carries the fault-injected rollback,
                                     the 2-then-10 concurrency round and the
                                     five-identical-key idempotency round
  All test source is VB (G-A)        already true
  <Assembly: DoNotParallelize>       already true, with its reasoning comment
  ADR-009 records the framework      ticked at P1-01

The genuinely open box was the WebApplicationFactory seam. Until now every
integration test called AuthService or StockService IN-PROCESS. That is the
right way to prove a transaction and it is structurally blind to routing,
model binding, the authentication handler, middleware order, and the error
envelope.

P1-10 already demonstrated that blindness has a cost. Before its fix, an
unauthenticated POST /api/v1/auth/login with an over-long X-Correlation-Id
returned HTTP 500 as text/plain carrying a MySqlException, a 25-frame stack
trace, the internal call chain, and absolute source paths from this disk.
Program.vb had registered no exception handler at all. No service-level test
could have seen it.

=============================================================================
THE VB OBSTACLE, AND WHY IT NEEDED A NEW TYPE
=============================================================================

Every C# example writes WebApplicationFactory<Program>. That does not
compile here, and the reason is a language rule rather than a missing
reference:

    error BC30371: Module 'Program' cannot be used as a type

Visual Basic's entry point is a MODULE (CLAUDE.md §3 — top-level statements
are C#-only, so Module Program / Sub Main is the only available shape). A VB
Module compiles to a sealed class carrying [StandardModule], and the
compiler refuses to let it be named as a type argument. Program is already
Public, so visibility was not the problem; this was confirmed by trying it,
not inferred.

WebApplicationFactory uses TEntryPoint only to locate the assembly whose
entry point builds the host, and to resolve the content root. It calls
nothing on the type. So Merchandising.Api.ApiEntryPoint was added: a public,
non-constructible, memberless marker.

The other common workaround is to name an existing controller as
TEntryPoint. Rejected: it makes the test seam depend on a controller that
someone may later rename or delete for unrelated reasons, and it explains
nothing to the next reader.

=============================================================================
THE STARTUP GUARD — A DELIBERATE CHANGE TO PRODUCTION CODE
=============================================================================

WebApplicationFactory replaces Kestrel with an in-memory TestServer, but it
still EXECUTES Main to build the host. Main called
CertificateOptionsLoader.Load() unconditionally, so a test host would have
tried to load a .pfx from %ProgramData%\MerchandisingSystem\config.

That file exists on this machine. The tests would therefore have passed
here and failed on any clean clone — the precise failure mode this project
keeps catching late and paying for.

Program.vb now skips the certificate load and the ConfigureKestrel block
when, and only when, the environment is "Testing":

    Public Const TestingEnvironmentName As String = "Testing"

    If Not builder.Environment.IsEnvironment(TestingEnvironmentName) Then
        ... CertificateOptionsLoader.Load() + ConfigureKestrel ...
    End If

Written as an explicit named-environment check rather than "not Production".
A missing certificate in Development, Staging or Production still fails the
process at boot, loudly, which is the behaviour P1-09 deliberately built.
Only a host with no Kestrel to configure is exempt.

DatabaseOptionsLoader is deliberately NOT guarded. Integration tests are
supposed to reach the real database (ADR-000), so the test host loads the
same database configuration a real host does.

=============================================================================
PACKAGE PINNED
=============================================================================

    Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing  10.0.9

Chosen to match Microsoft.AspNetCore.App 10.0.9, the runtime measured on
this machine (ADR-002/003). NuGet offered 10.0.0 through 10.0.11 at the time
of writing; 10.0.11 was available and deliberately not taken. CLAUDE.md §6:
never "latest". Recorded in the ADR pins table.

InternalsVisibleTo("Merchandising.Tests.Integration") was added to
Merchandising.Api and confirmed emitted into the generated AssemblyInfo.
Nothing depends on it today — Program is already Public — but the
alternative, when a test first needs a Friend member, is widening that
member to Public, which is the wrong direction.

=============================================================================
A TEST THAT WAS WRONG, AND WHAT IT TAUGHT
=============================================================================

The first draft asserted that the X-Non-Production-Http marker would be
ABSENT on the test host, reasoning that the host is not Development. It
failed. The assertion was wrong, not the code:
NonProductionWarningMiddleware never reads the environment — it tags any
request where Request.IsHttps is false. TestServer carries no TLS, so a
default client is a plain-HTTP request and MUST be tagged.

Keying on transport rather than environment is the stronger design: it tags
the actual risk instead of a configuration value someone can set wrongly.

Replaced with a discriminating pair, which proves more than the original
intended:

    PlainHttpRequest_IsTaggedNonProduction     http  -> tagged
    HttpsRequest_IsNotTaggedNonProduction      https -> not tagged

Without the second, the first would also pass for middleware that tagged
every response unconditionally — the bug that would make the marker
worthless. P1-09 proved this against live Kestrel listeners; this proves the
pipeline itself produces it, on a host with no certificate at all.

=============================================================================
A GUARDRAIL RACE, FOUND BY THIS CARD
=============================================================================

The first full run failed the BUILD, not a test:

    G-D  Checking Option Strict inheritance...
    Get-Content : ...\Merchandising.Inventory_x_wpftmp.vbproj ... PathNotFound
    error MSB3073: check-no-csharp.ps1 exited with code 1

Not a violation — a race. The WPF targets generate a temporary shim project
(Foo_x_wpftmp.vbproj) in the PROJECT directory, not under obj/, and delete it
moments later. check-no-csharp.ps1 also runs from Directory.Build.targets
DURING the build, so one WPF project can create and delete that file while
the scan is enumerating.

Latent before now; P1-15 gave Merchandising.Tests.Unit UseWPF, making a
fourth WPF project available to overlap with the API's build step.

Fixed by excluding *_wpftmp.vbproj from Get-SourceFiles. Safe: the shim is
generated FROM the real project file, which is still scanned, so a violation
cannot exist only in the shim. An intermittent guardrail failure that means
nothing is the fastest way to teach someone to ignore guardrails.

